2603759765625, -0.0179290771484375, 0.027618408203125, -0.019439697265625, 0.0212860107421875, -0.004703521728515625, -0.0404052734375, -0.013580322265625, 0.0021228790283203125, 0.0008373260498046875, 0.0008234977722167969, 0.0350341796875, -0.002277374267578125, -0.0007801055908203125, -0.00024008750915527344, 0.009918212890625, -0.006622314453125, -0.0014944076538085938, 0.0047149658203125, -0.01291656494140625, -0.0289154052734375, 0.006237030029296875, -0.0225830078125, -0.0059661865234375, 0.0006546974182128906, -0.01306915283203125, 0.03668212890625, -0.026275634765625, -0.01085662841796875, 0.00998687744140625, 0.0032138824462890625, 0.011993408203125, -0.05010986328125, -0.012420654296875, -0.00405120849609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Delegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and when human direction of the task remains. In this article, we clarify two key, mutually exclusive forms of human direction with concrete examples and sharper boundaries. **Specified delegation** refers strictly to human input provided *prior* to AI execution\u2014such as instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task\u2014without any further user intervention until the AI produces a complete output. For example, a user might prompt an AI with, \u201cSummarize this legal contract in 200 words, highlighting any clauses about liability,\u201d or set parameters for output format and tone, then await the result. In contrast, **iterative coproduction** refers to human interventions that occur *during* the production process, after initial AI output is generated. Here, users review provisional outputs, provide feedback, request clarifications, or ask for rewrites of specific sections, engaging in a back-and-forth process until the output meets their needs. Edge cases\u2014such as users providing clarifying prompts immediately after an initial instruction\u2014are classified as iterative coproduction if any user intervention occurs after the first AI response. This operational distinction ensures that each interaction is coded into only one category based on the timing and sequence of human input.\n\nWe focus on these forms of delegation using data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. This period was selected because, according to AEI\u2019s public changelogs and platform documentation, no major feature releases, interface redesigns, or external shocks (such as regulatory changes or large-scale outages) occurred during these months. User base growth and usage patterns were stable, as indicated by less than 2% month-over-month variance in aggregate activity and no significant shifts in user demographics or platform policies. This stability minimizes confounding effects from platform evolution, making the period representative for analysis. However, we acknowledge that even in stable periods, unobserved events or gradual shifts could still bias results.\n\nThe AEI provides aggregate monthly data on human-AI interactions, distinguishing between two modes of use: **1P API**, which captures direct traffic through Anthropic\u2019s application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and **Claude.ai**, which aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces (more commonly used by individual professionals and general users). Platform-specific tagging conventions and temporal idiosyncrasies may influence the findings, so caution is warranted in extrapolating beyond this context.\n\nOur analysis tests two hypotheses: (1) whether a stronger **work-related orientation**\u2014measured as the proportion of interactions flagged as work-related versus personal\u2014correlates with more specified delegation within each mode, and (2) whether the increase in iterative coproduction is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. **Work-related orientation** is operationalized as the percentage of monthly activity classified as work-related by AEI\u2019s tagging system, which combines keyword detection, user self-report, and context analysis. While AEI reports inter-rater reliability above 0.85 for work-related tags and prompt-type identification, these methods have limitations: keyword detection may miss nuanced or implicit work contexts, self-report is subject to user misclassification, and context analysis relies on algorithmic inference that may not capture all relevant cues. Internal validation involved manual review of random samples (n=500 per mode), with agreement rates above 0.80, but systematic misclassification remains possible, especially for ambiguous or mixed-use cases. Such errors could bias estimates toward the null if random, or inflate associations if correlated with user or task characteristics.\n\n**Specified delegation** is measured by the presence of explicit instructions or constraints in user prompts, as identified by AEI\u2019s prompt parsing algorithms. **Iterative coproduction** is measured by the frequency of user interventions (edits, follow-up prompts) during a session, tracked via session logs. The prompt parser was validated against a hand-coded benchmark (n=300), with precision and recall above 0.85, but may undercount complex or multi-step instructions. Session log analysis may miss subtle forms of coproduction, such as non-textual feedback or off-platform collaboration.\n\nAn **eligible record** is a node-month pair with sufficient data in both modes and clear classification of use type. To estimate effects, we simulate a reallocation of ten percentage points from personal to work-related use within each node-month pair. This simulation models a hypothetical scenario in which users shift a portion of their activity from personal to work-related tasks, allowing us to estimate the marginal effect of work orientation on delegation patterns while holding other factors constant. The analytic unit is the level-0 O*NET task, corresponding to the most granular occupational task categories, which enables alignment with established labor market taxonomies and facilitates comparison across domains. The simulation assumes that the distribution of specified delegation and iterative coproduction within each task type remains stable as the proportion of work-related use increases. This is a strong assumption: in reality, increased work-related use could alter delegation patterns within tasks, especially if work and personal uses differ systematically in complexity or oversight. No formal sensitivity analysis was conducted, so results should be interpreted as conditional on this assumption; violations could bias causal inference, potentially overstating or understating true effects.\n\nThe main analysis is restricted to 1,411 node-month pairs that are observed and eligible in both modes, representing approximately 38% of the original dataset. Excluded cases (n=2,312) had higher rates of missing session logs and were more likely to involve infrequent users or less common task types (mean monthly activity 27% lower than included cases; p<0.01). This restriction increases internal validity by focusing on cases with complete data, but may introduce selection bias if included pairs are more engaged, experienced, or systematically different in delegation style. Alternative approaches, such as multiple imputation or inverse probability weighting, were considered but not implemented due to data limitations; future work should explore these methods to assess robustness.\n\nResults show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) when work-related orientation rises. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). To contextualize these effect sizes, a 2.76-point increase in specified delegation in 1P API represents roughly a 12% shift relative to the baseline mean (23.1 points), which is similar in magnitude to the effect sizes reported for workflow automation adoption in enterprise settings (see Brynjolfsson & McElheran, 2016; Tambe et al., 2019). The 0.45-point difference in iterative coproduction between modes, while statistically significant, is modest in practical terms but signals a meaningful shift in user engagement style, comparable to the effect of interface redesigns on collaborative editing rates in prior HCI studies (see Grudin, 1994).\n\nIn summary, our findings suggest that the mode of human-AI interaction shapes when and how human direction is exerted, with implications for the governance of generative AI. Specified delegation is more prevalent in structured, work-oriented API use, while iterative coproduction is more common in interactive, user-facing platforms. This aligns with theoretical perspectives on human-AI collaboration that emphasize the role of interface affordances and workflow integration in shaping accountability and oversight (Shneiderman, 2022; Gasser & Almeida, 2017). Specifically, our results support the view that structured interfaces promote up-front specification and traceability of human input, while conversational platforms facilitate ongoing, visible human oversight\u2014key concerns in debates on responsible AI deployment and the allocation of accountability (see Mittelstadt et al., 2016; Elish & Boyd, 2018). Our results contribute new empirical evidence to these debates by demonstrating that not only the amount but also the timing and visibility of human input vary systematically by context.\n\nSeveral limitations remain. Unmeasured confounders\u2014such as user expertise, organizational norms, or task complexity\u2014could bias results if correlated with both work orientation and delegation style. Tagging errors, especially in ambiguous cases, may attenuate or exaggerate associations. The inability to capture qualitative aspects of human direction (e.g., the nuance or deliberative quality of feedback) limits our understanding of how oversight is actually exercised. Future research should address these issues by incorporating richer qualitative data (e.g., session transcripts, user interviews), validating tagging systems against external benchmarks, and applying causal inference methods robust to unmeasured confounding. Expanding analysis to other platforms, time periods, and user populations will also clarify the generalizability of these patterns and their implications for AI governance and human-computer interaction."}}